1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,643
Young man:

2
00:00:00,643 --> 00:00:06,930
That seminar yesterday on automation and the future of work was really good, wasn’t

3
00:00:06,930 --> 00:00:06,980
it?

4
00:00:06,980 --> 00:00:11,424
Looking at the first industrial revolution in Britain in the 19th century

5
00:00:11,424 --> 00:00:16,380
and seeing how people reacted to massive change was a real eye-opener.

6
00:00:16,380 --> 00:00:17,745
Young woman:

7
00:00:17,745 --> 00:00:17,845
Yes.

8
00:00:17,845 --> 00:00:21,236
It was interesting to hear how people felt about automation then

9
00:00:21,236 --> 00:00:22,971
and what challenges they faced.

10
00:00:22,971 --> 00:00:27,480
I didn’t know that first started with workers in the textile industry.

11
00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:28,219
Young man:

12
00:00:28,219 --> 00:00:33,393
With those protesting workers called the Luddites destroying their knitting machines because they were

13
00:00:33,393 --> 00:00:35,610
so worried about losing their jobs.

14
00:00:35,610 --> 00:00:36,438
Young woman:

15
00:00:36,438 --> 00:00:36,853
Yes,

16
00:00:36,853 --> 00:00:39,752
and ultimately, they didn’t achieve anything.

17
00:00:39,752 --> 00:00:43,480
And anyway, industrialisation created more jobs than it destroyed.

18
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:44,073
Young man:

19
00:00:44,073 --> 00:00:44,369
Yes,

20
00:00:44,369 --> 00:00:48,517
that’s true – but it probably didn’t seem a positive thing at the

21
00:00:48,517 --> 00:00:48,813
time.

22
00:00:48,813 --> 00:00:51,480
I can see why the Luddites felt so threatened.

23
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:52,119
Young woman:

24
00:00:52,119 --> 00:00:52,758
I know.

25
00:00:52,758 --> 00:00:55,635
I’m sure I would have felt the same.

26
00:00:55,635 --> 00:00:59,790
The discussion about the future of work was really optimistic for a change.

27
00:00:59,790 --> 00:01:05,297
I like the idea that work won’t involve doing boring, repetitive tasks, as robots

28
00:01:05,297 --> 00:01:06,766
will do all that.

29
00:01:06,766 --> 00:01:10,070
Normally, you only hear negative stuff about the future.

30
00:01:10,070 --> 00:01:10,678
Young man:

31
00:01:10,678 --> 00:01:12,804
Bit too optimistic, don’t you think?

32
00:01:12,804 --> 00:01:17,057
For example, I can’t see how people are about to have more leisure

33
00:01:17,057 --> 00:01:17,361
time,

34
00:01:17,361 --> 00:01:21,310
when all the evidence shows people are spending longer than ever at work.

35
00:01:21,310 --> 00:01:21,964
Young woman:

36
00:01:21,964 --> 00:01:23,273
No – that’s true.

37
00:01:23,273 --> 00:01:24,909
And what about lower unemployment?

38
00:01:24,909 --> 00:01:27,200
I’m not so sure about that.

39
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:27,848
Young man:

40
00:01:27,848 --> 00:01:31,410
Perhaps in the long term – but not in the foreseeable future.

41
00:01:31,410 --> 00:01:31,975
Young woman:

42
00:01:31,975 --> 00:01:32,258
Mmm.

43
00:01:32,258 --> 00:01:36,495
And I expect most people will be expected to work until they’re much older

44
00:01:36,495 --> 00:01:38,190
– as everyone’s living much longer.

45
00:01:38,190 --> 00:01:39,273
Young man:

46
00:01:39,273 --> 00:01:41,440
That’s already happening.

47
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:41,947
Young woman:

48
00:01:41,947 --> 00:01:44,990
I enjoyed all that stuff on how technology has changed some jobs

49
00:01:44,990 --> 00:01:47,780
and how they’re likely to change in the near future.

50
00:01:47,780 --> 00:01:48,460
Young man:

51
00:01:48,460 --> 00:01:49,139
Yeah, incredible.

52
00:01:49,139 --> 00:01:49,819
Like accountants.

53
00:01:49,819 --> 00:01:54,575
You might think all the technological innovations would have put them out of a

54
00:01:54,575 --> 00:01:54,915
job,

55
00:01:54,915 --> 00:01:58,313
but in fact there are more of them than ever.

56
00:01:58,313 --> 00:02:02,390
They’re still really in demand and have become far more efficient.

57
00:02:02,390 --> 00:02:03,137
Young woman:

58
00:02:03,137 --> 00:02:03,510
Right.

59
00:02:03,510 --> 00:02:04,630
That was amazing.

60
00:02:04,630 --> 00:02:09,110
Twenty times more accountants in this country compared to the 19th century.

61
00:02:09,110 --> 00:02:09,824
Young man:

62
00:02:09,824 --> 00:02:10,538
I know.

63
00:02:10,538 --> 00:02:15,181
I’d never have thought that demand for hairdressing would have gone up

64
00:02:15,181 --> 00:02:17,681
so much in the last hundred years.

65
00:02:17,681 --> 00:02:22,680
One hairdresser for every 287 people now, compared to one for over 1,500.

66
00:02:22,680 --> 00:02:23,243
Young woman:

67
00:02:23,243 --> 00:02:27,470
Yeah because people’s earning power has gone up so they can afford to spend

68
00:02:27,470 --> 00:02:29,160
more on personal services like that.

69
00:02:29,160 --> 00:02:29,763
Young man:

70
00:02:29,763 --> 00:02:32,780
But technology hasn’t changed the actual job that much.

71
00:02:32,780 --> 00:02:33,346
Young woman:

72
00:02:33,346 --> 00:02:35,043
No, they’ve got hairdryers, etc.

73
00:02:35,043 --> 00:02:38,720
but it’s one job where you don’t depend on a computer …

74
00:02:38,720 --> 00:02:44,219
The kind of work that administrative staff do has changed enormously, thanks to technology.

75
00:02:44,219 --> 00:02:48,540
Even 20 years ago there were secretaries doing dictation and typing.

76
00:02:48,540 --> 00:02:49,267
Young man:

77
00:02:49,267 --> 00:02:49,631
Yes.

78
00:02:49,631 --> 00:02:51,813
Really boring compared to these days,

79
00:02:51,813 --> 00:02:55,450
when they’re given much more responsibility and higher status.

80
00:02:55,450 --> 00:02:56,022
Young woman:

81
00:02:56,022 --> 00:02:56,308
Mmm.

82
00:02:56,308 --> 00:03:00,312
A lot of graduates go in for this kind of work now … I’d

83
00:03:00,312 --> 00:03:04,316
expected there to be a much bigger change in the number of agricultural workers

84
00:03:04,316 --> 00:03:05,460
in the 19th century.

85
00:03:05,460 --> 00:03:11,415
But the 1871 census showed that roughly 25% of the population worked on the

86
00:03:11,415 --> 00:03:11,840
land.

87
00:03:11,840 --> 00:03:12,413
Young man:

88
00:03:12,413 --> 00:03:15,564
Yeah, I’d have assumed it would be more than 50%.

89
00:03:15,564 --> 00:03:17,570
Now it’s less than 0.2%.

90
00:03:17,570 --> 00:03:18,280
Young woman:

91
00:03:18,280 --> 00:03:19,700
What about care workers?

92
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:20,370
Young man:

93
00:03:20,370 --> 00:03:24,387
They barely existed in the 19th century as people’s lifespan was

94
00:03:24,387 --> 00:03:25,392
so much shorter.

95
00:03:25,392 --> 00:03:28,740
But now of course this sector will see huge growth.

96
00:03:28,740 --> 00:03:29,405
Young woman:

97
00:03:29,405 --> 00:03:32,730
Yeah – and it’s hard enough to meet current demand.

98
00:03:32,730 --> 00:03:35,390
The future looks quite bleak for bank clerks.

99
00:03:35,390 --> 00:03:39,380
They’ve been in decline since ATMs were introduced in the eighties.

100
00:03:39,380 --> 00:03:39,949
Young man:

101
00:03:39,949 --> 00:03:43,935
And technology will certainly make most of the jobs they do now redundant, I

102
00:03:43,935 --> 00:03:44,220
think.。

103
00:03:44,220 --> 00:03:44,864
Young woman:

104
00:03:44,864 --> 00:03:47,120
I agree, although the situation may change.

105
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:50,020
It’s very hard to predict what will happen.
